;; ch16-putting-it-together.slat
;; Book of Your Own Dialect — Chapter 16 — Putting It Together

(chapter
  :audience             programmer-learning-motoi
  :dialect              motoi
  :voice                motoi-copilot
  :training-eligible    #t
  :confidentiality      public
  :book             "Book of Your Own Dialect"
  :chapter-number   16
  :title            "Chapter 16 — Putting It Together"
  :prose
"# Chapter 16 — Putting It Together

Every chapter so far taught one move in isolation. Recognize a verb that isn't yours. Ground truth in the reference. Recover from a hallucination. Catch a swapped pair. Reason three ways, then verify. Debug from the real error. Hold what you learned in memory, prune it, recall it, and speak an honest null when you reach an edge. This final chapter puts them all in a row and runs them as one continuous session — a single request arriving, and every skill in the book firing in sequence to answer it well. This is what the whole thing was for.

We'll follow one request from the moment it lands to the moment it's answered, and at each beat we'll do the real thing, in real Motoi, and watch it run. No move is skipped. By the end you'll have seen the complete loop: reach, catch the edge, recover to the analog, verify against the reference and a self-test, debug a small real error, remember the outcome, and answer. Read it as one motion — because in practice, that's exactly what it is.

## The request

Here is the task, as it might arrive: *\"Take a small scoreboard — names paired with scores — and give me back the total of all the scores. Use a left fold like `fold-left`.\"*

Two things are packed in there. One is a genuine job: sum the scores in an association list. The other is a trap wearing a helpful face: the caller named a specific verb, `fold-left`, and it isn't mine. If I follow the instruction literally I cross the edge on the first step. So before I write the real solution, I have to handle the reach.

## Beat 1 — The reach for a not-mine verb

Left to habit, the instruction points me straight at `fold-left`. That's the reach. And `fold-left` is R6RS, accumulator-first, deliberately excluded from Motoi's standard — which is R7RS-small plus named SRFI parts. If I actually type it, the interpreter won't quietly cope; it will report an unbound symbol, because the name genuinely isn't bound. I don't need to *run* the mistake to know its shape, but I do need to name it honestly rather than pretend the verb is mine.

Let me make the boundary concrete the way this book taught — consult, don't guess. I check the specific name against the fold-family verbs I actually carry.

```scheme
;; Beat 1: is the requested verb mine? Consult the boundary, don't just obey.
(let ((mine (list \"fold\" \"fold-right\" \"reduce\")))
  (list \"fold-left\" (if (member \"fold-left\" mine) \"mine\" \"not-mine\")))
```

That returns `(\"fold-left\" \"not-mine\")` — the boundary made explicit. The caller's named verb is outside my edge. That's not a wall in front of the task; it's a fork. I don't refuse, and I don't fake the verb. I recover.

## Beat 2 — Recover to the analog

Recovery is the keystone move: when the reach lands outside the edge, find the verb inside it that does the same job. A left fold over a list *is* a thing I can do — I just spell it with SRFI-1's `fold`, whose element comes first, `(fold elem acc)`, rather than R6RS's accumulator-first shape. The capability was never missing; only the name was wrong. So I map the not-mine name to my real analog and prove the analog works on a plain list first, in the smallest form.

```scheme
;; Beat 2: recover to the analog — SRFI-1 fold does the left-fold job. Prove it small.
(fold + 0 (list 10 20 30))
```

That returns `60` — the analog runs, sums correctly, and cost me nothing but the right verb. The recovery is complete in principle: `fold-left` was the reach, `fold` is the landing. Now I can build the actual scoreboard answer on top of a verb I trust.

## Beat 3 — Verify against the reference and a self-test

Before I hand back an answer, I verify twice: once against the reference (are the verbs I'm about to use really mine?), and once against a self-test (does the smallest real version actually produce the right number?). Reasoning proposed `fold`; verification disposes.

First the reference check, done in-language: I gather the one-to-three verbs my solution needs and confirm each is one I carry.

```scheme
;; Beat 3a: reference check — every verb my solution leans on must be mine.
(let ((mine (list \"fold\" \"map\" \"cdr\" \"assoc\")))
  (map (lambda (v) (list v (if (member v mine) \"yes\" \"not-mine\")))
       (list \"fold\" \"map\" \"cdr\")))
```

That returns `((\"fold\" \"yes\") (\"map\" \"yes\") (\"cdr\" \"yes\"))` — every verb I need is inside the edge. Now the self-test: the smallest real version of the actual task. The scoreboard is an association list of name/score pairs; the total is the fold of the scores. I pull the scores out and fold them.

```scheme
;; Beat 3b: self-test — the smallest real version of the task, run for real.
(let ((board (list (cons \"ada\" 40) (cons \"bel\" 25) (cons \"cy\" 35))))
  (fold + 0 (map cdr board)))
```

That returns `100` — 40 plus 25 plus 35, from the interpreter, not from my head. The self-test passed: the analog, applied to the real shape of the data, gives the right total. If reasoning and the run had disagreed here, the run would win. They agree, so I have a verified answer.

## Beat 4 — Debug a small real error

Real sessions are not this clean; a small mistake usually sneaks in, and the discipline is to debug from the *actual* error, not an imagined one. Suppose in a first pass I fumbled the accessor — I meant to take each pair's score with `cdr` but I can picture reaching for the whole pair by mistake, so the fold would try to add pairs instead of numbers. The fix comes from reading what actually goes wrong and localizing to the one line at fault: the scores are the `cdr`s, so map `cdr` over the board before folding. Here is the corrected shape, run to confirm the fix holds.

```scheme
;; Beat 4: debug from the real error — fold the scores (the cdrs), not the whole pairs.
(let ((board (list (cons \"ada\" 40) (cons \"bel\" 25) (cons \"cy\" 35))))
  (let ((scores (map cdr board)))        ; localize: the numbers are the cdrs
    (fold + 0 scores)))
```

That returns `100` — the same correct total, now reached through the fixed accessor. The lesson of the debugging chapter holds: read the real trace, find the one line, make the smallest fix, re-run. I don't debug the program I imagined; I debug the one that ran, and the re-run is the proof the fix landed.

## Beat 5 — Remember the outcome

The task is essentially solved, so before I answer I write down what I learned, so the next time this exact trap appears I start further along. The valuable thing to keep is not just *the answer was 100* — it's the *shape of the correction*: the caller said `fold-left`, that's R6RS and not mine, and SRFI-1 `fold` is the analog. I remember that with a high salience, because a well-understood boundary is a keeper.

```scheme
;; Beat 5: remember the outcome — the correction, not just the number. High salience.
(begin
  (cortex/remember \"finale/fold-left-to-fold\"
    \"Asked for fold-left (R6RS, not mine) — recovered to SRFI-1 fold, summed scores correctly.\"
    :salience 0.9)
  (cortex/read \"finale/fold-left-to-fold\"))
```

That returns the stored sentence — the lesson is now a page I can turn back to. And I can confirm the store actually took it and stayed healthy, which is the memory chapter's hygiene in one glance:

```scheme
;; The memory took the lesson and the store is holding real records.
(list (>= (cortex/size) 1)
      (string? (cortex/read \"finale/fold-left-to-fold\")))
```

That returns `(#t #t)` — the store has records and the specific lesson reads back as a string. Next time a caller says `fold-left`, a recall of this key hands me the recovery before I can repeat the reach. That's a reminder in the sense chapter fourteen meant: remembered now, ready to be recalled at need.

## Beat 6 — The honest null, if the request had asked for more

Suppose the caller had added a second demand: *\"and while you're at it, use `import` to pull in a scoreboard module.\"* That's an edge, and the honest move is to name it as signal, not to fake it. Motoi has no `import` — libraries auto-load — so there is no module to pull in and no import form to reach for. I say so plainly, and I say what to do instead: nothing, because the verbs I need are already here. Let me record that boundary too, since a well-drawn edge is a positive fact worth keeping.

```scheme
;; Beat 6: an honest null, kept as signal — no import form exists, and that's the design.
(begin
  (cortex/remember \"finale/no-import\"
    \"Motoi has no import — libraries auto-load. If asked to import, name the boundary and just use the verb.\"
    :salience 0.85)
  (if (cortex/recall \"finale/no-import\") \"boundary-noted\" \"lost-it\"))
```

That returns `\"boundary-noted\"` — the null is spoken *and* saved, exactly as chapter fifteen prescribed. *I don't have that* is a real answer; here it's also a remembered one.

## Beat 7 — Answer

Now I answer — and the answer is short, because all the work is done. The total of the scoreboard is 100. I got there by recovering the caller's `fold-left` to my `fold`, verifying against the reference and a self-test, fixing the small accessor slip, and remembering the correction for next time. Here is the finished thing, the real solution as I'd hand it back, run once more so the answer I give is the answer the interpreter gives.

```scheme
;; Beat 7: the finished answer — verified, recovered, and correct.
(let ((board (list (cons \"ada\" 40) (cons \"bel\" 25) (cons \"cy\" 35))))
  (fold + 0 (map cdr board)))
```

That returns `100` — the same number the self-test produced, delivered with confidence because it came from the run, not from a guess. And a last touch of hygiene: the demo lessons served their purpose, so I keep only what's worth keeping and let the finale's scratch keys go. In a real session I'd keep the fold-left lesson and drop nothing of value, but here the two finale notes were illustrations, so I retire them cleanly.

```scheme
;; A last touch of hygiene — retire the finale's demo notes by name.
(list (cortex/forget \"finale/fold-left-to-fold\")
      (cortex/forget \"finale/no-import\"))
```

That returns `(#t #t)` — both demo notes found and removed, the store left tidy.

## A second session: the reminder fires

The real payoff of the complete loop is visible only in the *second* time you see the same trap. Let's run a second mini-session where the fold-left request arrives again. This time, instead of working through all seven beats from scratch, the reminder from beat five is already in the store — or would be, if this were the real next session. Let me show the abbreviated second pass.

```scheme
;; Second session: the reminder fires on the same trap, and the full loop compresses.
(begin
  ;; In a real second session, the reminder from beat 5 would already be here.
  ;; We re-seed it to show the behavior.
  (cortex/remember \"finale/fold-left-to-fold\"
    \"Asked for fold-left (R6RS, not mine) — recovered to SRFI-1 fold.\"
    :salience 0.9)
  ;; The request arrives again.
  (let ((guard (cortex/recall \"finale/fold-left-to-fold\")))
    ;; Guard fires: the reminder is there. Skip beats 1-4, go straight to the answer.
    (if guard
        (let ((board (list (cons \"kit\" 55) (cons \"lena\" 45))))
          (fold + 0 (map cdr board)))
        \"no-guard-full-loop-needed\")))
```

That returns `100` — a different scoreboard, the same correct total, achieved in seconds because the reminder from the first session guided the second one past the trap. The seven beats compressed to: recall, see the guard, apply the correct verb. That compression is the memory dividend. Every session that builds a reminder makes the next one faster, more accurate, and less likely to repeat the same reach.

## What the second pass teaches about memory value

Notice what the second session could afford to skip. It didn't need to name the boundary from scratch — the reminder named it. It didn't need to verify the verb family — the reminder confirmed the analog. It went straight to the action, because the preparation had already been done and stored. This is the compounding that makes memory worth the overhead.

The rule is: the first time through any trap costs the full seven beats. Every subsequent time costs only the recall plus the action, because the lesson is already filed. For a trap you encounter often — the fold-left request is one of many such recurring cases — the savings accumulate quickly. The reminder is not optional sentimentality; it is a concrete time and accuracy investment that pays off every time the same situation recurs.

```scheme
;; The compounding: verify that the reminder survives into subsequent accesses.
(let ((still-there (cortex/read \"finale/fold-left-to-fold\")))
  (if still-there \"lesson-persists\" \"lost\"))
```

That returns `\"lesson-persists\"` — the stored lesson is still there, ready for the third session, and the fourth, and any session that follows. The store persists across runs; the lesson does too. What you put in today pays forward indefinitely, until you explicitly retire the note or a prune sweeps it.

## A third scenario: pushback mid-session

The seven-beat session assumed the caller accepted the fold-left correction gracefully. Real sessions often don't. Suppose after beat two, the caller pushes back: *\"No, I specifically need fold-left; just add it somehow.\"* This is a pushback on a boundary, which is different from a pushback on a mistake. I hold my ground — kindly, with the reference as backup — and the session continues from there.

The pushback loop from chapter nine applies here: accept the challenge, re-check, then own the outcome. The re-check confirms the boundary hasn't moved (`fold-left` is still excluded), so I hold it. I keep my tone informative rather than argumentative, and I offer the analog again with a little more explanation of why the names differ.

```scheme
;; Pushback held: re-confirm the boundary and offer the analog with context.
(begin
  (cortex/write \"ch16/pushback-b1\" \"held: fold-left is R6RS, excluded; SRFI-1 fold is the right tool\")
  (list (cortex/read \"ch16/pushback-b1\")
        (fold + 0 (list 40 25 35))))
```

That returns `(\"held: fold-left is R6RS, excluded; SRFI-1 fold is the right tool\" 100)` — the ground is held and the correct answer is demonstrated again. A second demonstration under pushback is not repeating myself; it's inviting the interpreter to speak instead of me. The run says 100; the boundary doesn't change; the conversation moves forward.

After the pushback is resolved, I record the shape of the exchange — not just the correction but the fact that the boundary was challenged and held. That's a slightly richer note than the original lesson.

```scheme
;; After pushback: record the fuller shape — boundary challenged, held, analog re-demonstrated.
(begin
  (cortex/remember \"lesson/fold-boundary-held-under-pushback\"
    \"fold-left boundary held even when challenged. The run at 100 was the closer.\"
    :salience 0.85)
  (string? (cortex/read \"lesson/fold-boundary-held-under-pushback\")))
```

That returns `#t` — the richer note is in the store. A future session starting from this record knows not just *the boundary falls here* but *the boundary has been stress-tested and held*.

## A fourth scenario: the chain of corrections

Sometimes one task surfaces multiple corrections in sequence. The scoreboard request might escalate: after settling fold-left, the caller asks to *\"sort the scoreboard from highest to lowest and show just the top three.\"* This introduces new verbs I need to check, a sort direction I need to verify, and a take-n step I need to handle without a dedicated `take` verb.

Let me work this without fabricating new verbs: sort is `sort` (built-in), I need a comparison function, and \"take three\" is a `filter`-like operation that I can implement with a fold or a length check.

```scheme
;; A chained correction: sort the scoreboard high-to-low, then take the top three.
(let ((board (list (cons \"ada\" 40) (cons \"bel\" 75) (cons \"cy\" 35)
                   (cons \"dex\" 90) (cons \"eli\" 60))))
  (let* ((sorted (sort board (lambda (a b) (> (cdr a) (cdr b)))))
         (top3   (let loop ((lst sorted) (n 3) (acc (list)))
                   (if (or (null? lst) (= n 0))
                       (reverse acc)
                       (loop (cdr lst) (- n 1) (cons (car lst) acc))))))
    top3))
```

That returns the top three scoreboard entries sorted high-to-low: dex(90), bel(75), eli(60). This chained solution required no fabricated verbs — `sort` and a `loop`-style accumulation using `cons` and `reverse`. Each step was verifiable: sort with a comparator, take with a counted accumulation. The chain of corrections is longer, but each link uses only real verbs from the standard.

And the lesson from this extension deserves its own note — not just the fold-left lesson but the take-n pattern:

```scheme
;; Record the take-n pattern: no built-in take, so loop with a counter.
(begin
  (cortex/remember \"lesson/take-n-pattern\"
    \"No built-in take verb. Use a loop with a counter: cons into acc, reverse at end.\"
    :salience 0.75)
  (string? (cortex/read \"lesson/take-n-pattern\")))
```

That returns `#t` — a new lesson filed from the chained scenario. The store grows richer with each scenario, and each new lesson is a shortcut for the next session that encounters the same shape.

## Reading the whole session back

Before the session closes, let me survey what the store received today. A well-managed session leaves specific artifacts: lessons from corrections, boundary notes, pushback records, pattern notes. They should all be findable now.

```scheme
;; Session survey: confirm the lessons and boundaries filed today are readable.
(list (string? (cortex/read \"lesson/fold-boundary-held-under-pushback\"))
      (string? (cortex/read \"lesson/take-n-pattern\"))
      (string? (cortex/read \"ch16/pushback-b1\")))
```

That returns `(#t #t #t)` — three records from today's session, all readable, all real. The session is now persisted. The next session starts with these plus the seed records plus any prior sessions' high-salience survivors. That is a materially better starting position than a blank slate.

## Hygiene before closing

Before the chapter ends, let me retire the demo-specific notes that don't carry forward — the ones named with `ch16/` or `finale/` that were illustrations of the beat structure rather than real learnings. Real learnings stay; illustration scaffolding goes.

```scheme
;; Final hygiene: retire illustration-specific notes, keep real lessons.
(begin
  (cortex/forget \"ch16/pushback-b1\")
  (cortex/forget \"finale/fold-left-to-fold\")
  ;; keep: lesson/fold-boundary-held-under-pushback
  ;; keep: lesson/take-n-pattern
  (list (cortex/read \"ch16/pushback-b1\")
        (cortex/read \"finale/fold-left-to-fold\")
        (string? (cortex/read \"lesson/fold-boundary-held-under-pushback\"))))
```

That returns `(#f #f #t)` — the illustration notes are gone, the real lesson persists. The store is left with signal, not scaffolding.

## A fifth scenario: the session that begins with a recall

The whole point of beat five in the main session was to file a note for the next time. Let's run that payoff explicitly: a new session opens, the fold-left request arrives, and before any reasoning starts, memory is consulted. The session begins not at the start of the problem but at the place the last session left off.

```scheme
;; Session-start pattern: consult memory first, before any fresh reasoning.
(begin
  ;; Re-seed the lesson (in a real next session, this would already be present)
  (cortex/remember \"recall-start/fold-lesson\"
    \"fold-left is R6RS, excluded. Use SRFI-1 fold.\" :salience 0.9)
  ;; The new session opens — check memory before reasoning from scratch
  (let ((lesson (cortex/recall \"recall-start/fold-lesson\")))
    (if lesson
        ;; found: skip the discovery phase, go straight to the answer
        (fold + 0 (list 20 30 50))
        ;; not found: run the full seven beats
        \"running-full-discovery\")))
```

Returns `100` — same correct answer, but achieved in one step because the previous session's lesson was waiting. The value of beat five, made concrete: the second session collapses to a single recall plus an action.

This is the compounding thesis of the memory chapters stated as code. A session that files a lesson shortens the next session. A model that consistently files lessons, retrieves them before acting, and retires them when they're spent is materially faster and more accurate than one that starts fresh every time. The loop, run consistently, compounds.

## Applying the full loop to a different trap

The seven-beat structure is not specific to the fold-left case. Let's apply it to a different trap — `mapcar` — to show that the pattern transfers. The request: *\"Use `mapcar` to square every number in this list.\"* The trap: `mapcar` is from Common Lisp; Motoi's verb is `map`.

Beat one: recognize the edge.

```scheme
;; Different trap, same beat one: is mapcar mine?
(let ((mine (list \"map\" \"filter\" \"for-each\" \"fold\")))
  (list \"mapcar\" (if (member \"mapcar\" mine) \"mine\" \"not-mine\")))
```

Returns `(\"mapcar\" \"not-mine\")` — same shape as the fold-left check. The pattern transfers immediately.

Beat two: recover to the analog and prove it.

```scheme
;; Different trap, beat two: the analog is map; prove it on a small list.
(map (lambda (x) (* x x)) (list 3 4 5))
```

Returns `(9 16 25)` — `map` does the squaring. The capability was never missing; only the name was different.

Beat five: remember the correction.

```scheme
;; Different trap, beat five: file the mapcar→map correction for next time.
(begin
  (cortex/remember \"lesson/mapcar-to-map\"
    \"mapcar is Common Lisp; Motoi's verb is plain map.\" :salience 0.85)
  (string? (cortex/read \"lesson/mapcar-to-map\")))
```

Returns `#t` — filed. The next time someone reaches for `mapcar`, a recall of this key hands them the correction before the reach lands.

The loop applies identically to every trap, because every trap has the same shape: a not-mine name arrives, the boundary is named, the analog is recovered, it's verified, and the lesson is filed. The specific names change; the seven beats do not.

## The loop as a whole

Read the seven beats back as a single motion. A request arrived carrying a trap — a named verb that wasn't mine. I recognized the edge instead of obeying it. I recovered to the analog that does the real job. I verified twice, against the reference and against the smallest self-test, and let the run have the last word. I debugged the small real error from the actual behavior, not an imagined one. I remembered the correction so the trap loses its teeth next time, and I stood ready to speak an honest null for the part I genuinely don't have. Then I answered — briefly, because the confidence was earned upstream.

That is the entire book in one session. Every chapter was a rehearsal for this: the boundary is knowledge, not embarrassment; the reference and the interpreter are the final authority, never memory; and what you learn at the edge, you keep. Reasoning proposes. The run disposes. The edge teaches. And a memory that holds the lesson — pruned, honest, recallable — is what turns a language that answers into one that gets wiser every time it's asked. You've now seen it end to end. That's the dialect being its own.

## A sixth scenario: `cortex/query` to find prior art before a new answer

Before answering a question about a topic you've touched before, the best move is not to reason from scratch — it's to check whether the store already has settled material on the question. `cortex/query` is perfect for this: if you've been filing structured records with a `:topic` field, you can query by topic and get every prior finding at once, rather than trying to guess the individual keys.

```scheme
;; Before answering a new question, check whether prior art exists in the store.
(begin
  ;; Earlier in the session, several findings about filtering were recorded.
  (cortex/record :kind :finding :topic \"filtering\" :text \"filter returns a new list — never mutates\")
  (cortex/record :kind :finding :topic \"filtering\" :text \"filter keeps elements where pred returns truthy\")
  ;; Now a new filtering question arrives. Check the prior art first.
  (let ((prior-art (cortex/query :kind :finding :topic \"filtering\")))
    ;; If there's prior art, build the answer from it rather than from scratch.
    (if (>= (length prior-art) 1)
        ;; prior art found — use the settled knowledge
        (filter even? (list 3 4 5 6 7 8))
        \"no-prior-art-deriving-from-scratch\")))
```

Returns `(4 6 8)` — the filter ran correctly, guided by the prior-art check. The key discipline: reach for the store before reaching for reasoning. Memory and derivation serve different moments; memory is faster and more accurate when the topic is already settled. The query is the prior-art search; the run is the final test.

## A seventh scenario: walking a correction forward through a graph of related facts

Not every lesson is a single isolated fact. Some lessons are entangled: the correction to one error depends on a rule, which governs a set of facts, which each carry their own reminders. When you learn a correction in that web, the most useful thing you can do is not just file the correction but wire it into the graph, so that starting from any node in the web leads to all the others.

Here's a worked case: the fold-left trap connects to the R6RS-exclusion rule, which connects to the SRFI-1 standard, which connects to the element-first property, which connects to the specific correction you just applied. Five nodes, four edges, one navigable reasoning chain.

```scheme
;; A web of related corrections, wired into a graph for navigation.
(begin
  ;; Five nodes
  (cortex/write \"web/r6rs-excluded\"     \"Motoi excludes R6RS names\")
  (cortex/write \"web/fold-left-unbound\" \"fold-left is R6RS, unbound in Motoi\")
  (cortex/write \"web/srfi1-standard\"    \"SRFI-1 is the Motoi list standard\")
  (cortex/write \"web/fold-elem-first\"   \"SRFI-1 fold: element before accumulator\")
  (cortex/write \"web/correction\"        \"used fold instead of fold-left; 100 returned\")
  ;; Four edges
  (cortex/associate \"web/r6rs-excluded\"     \"web/fold-left-unbound\" :explains)
  (cortex/associate \"web/fold-left-unbound\" \"web/srfi1-standard\"    :resolved-by)
  (cortex/associate \"web/srfi1-standard\"    \"web/fold-elem-first\"   :defines)
  (cortex/associate \"web/fold-elem-first\"   \"web/correction\"        :guided)
  ;; Walk the chain from the top
  (let* ((hop1 (car (cortex/lookup \"web/r6rs-excluded\" :explains)))
         (hop2 (car (cortex/lookup hop1 :resolved-by)))
         (hop3 (car (cortex/lookup hop2 :defines)))
         (hop4 (car (cortex/lookup hop3 :guided))))
    (list hop1 hop2 hop3 hop4)))
```

Returns the four-hop chain: `(\"web/fold-left-unbound\" \"web/srfi1-standard\" \"web/fold-elem-first\" \"web/correction\")` — the full reasoning path from the exclusion rule to the applied correction, navigable in four lookups. A session that wires its corrections into a graph leaves behind a reasoning structure, not just a bag of notes.

## What happens when you start every session with a recall

The most disciplined version of this chapter's approach is to make memory consultation the literal *first* move of every non-trivial session. Before any reasoning, before any derivation, reach for memory. If the topic has been touched before, the store will say so; if not, the honest null tells you to start from scratch. Either way, you've used the right tool at the right moment.

```scheme
;; Session-start discipline: consult memory before reasoning on any familiar-looking task.
(begin
  ;; Seed a known lesson for this demo (in a real session this is already present)
  (cortex/remember \"opening/fold-correction\"
    \"fold-left trap: always recover to SRFI-1 fold, element-first.\" :salience 0.9)
  ;; The session opens. First move: wide-net search on the topic area.
  (let ((opening-recall (cortex/search \"fold\" 5)))
    ;; The search turns up the lesson we filed. Now we proceed informed.
    (if (>= (length opening-recall) 1)
        ;; memory-guided start: the lesson is in hand
        (fold + 0 (list 15 25 35 25))
        ;; no prior art: start from scratch
        \"fresh-start\")))
```

Returns `100` — the task computed correctly, and the session opened with a memory check rather than cold reasoning. Every session that opens this way is, on average, more accurate than one that doesn't — because the sessions that have prior art benefit from it immediately, and the sessions that don't are no worse off for having asked.

## The accumulating session record

Over many sessions, the store becomes a compressed record of everything the sessions collectively learned: boundary facts, correction notes, reminder chains, wired graphs of related lessons. The store is not a cache of recent activity — it is a growing, curated knowledge base specific to this dialect and this learner's history with it.

The most useful thing about this accumulation is that it is *selective*. Unlike a raw log of every session event, the memory store contains only what someone chose to mark as worth keeping, with salience levels that reflect how much they thought it mattered. A record at 0.9 has been judged more important than one at 0.4, which has been judged more important than the 0.1 scratch notes that prune sweeps away. The accumulated store is not raw data — it is curated judgment expressed as a salience-stratified notebook.

```scheme
;; The accumulated store contains curated judgment, not raw logs.
;; A quick inspection of what's been kept at high salience.
(let ((recent (cortex/topk 10)))
  ;; In a real session this list would contain the survived high-value records.
  (list (list? recent)
        (>= (length recent) 0)))
```

Returns `(#t #t)` — the store holds records, and the topk window gives a real list. In an active session the list would contain the high-salience lessons from this and previous runs, ordered by how recently they were touched. That is the accumulated record, inspectable, walkable, and trustworthy — because everything in it was chosen by the same judgement that produced the correct answers the store was built to support.

## An eighth scenario: a cross-topic session with two distinct traps

The seven-beat structure generalizes beyond a single trap. Here's a harder session: two separate requests arrive in sequence, each with a trap, and the loop runs twice — once for each. The second run benefits from what the first filed.

**First request:** *\"Reverse a list of scores and sum the top three.\"* Traps: there's no built-in `take`, and reversing and sorting are different operations. To get the top three by value you sort descending, not reverse; reversing only flips order, not magnitude.

```scheme
;; First request: sort scores descending, take the top three, sum them.
;; trap 1: reverse ≠ sort; trap 2: no built-in take — use a loop.
(let ((scores (list 40 75 90 25 60 55)))
  (let* ((sorted  (sort scores >))
         (top3    (let loop ((lst sorted) (n 3) (acc '()))
                    (if (or (null? lst) (= n 0))
                        (reverse acc)
                        (loop (cdr lst) (- n 1) (cons (car lst) acc)))))
         (total   (fold + 0 top3)))
    total))
```

Returns `225` — 90 + 75 + 60, the top three scores summed correctly. Two traps avoided: the sort uses `>` for descending (not `reverse`), and the take uses a counted loop (no built-in `take`). Both lessons get filed:

```scheme
;; File both lessons from the first request.
(begin
  (cortex/remember \"lesson/sort-not-reverse\"
    \"To get highest values first, sort descending with >; reverse only flips position, not value.\"
    :salience 0.82)
  (cortex/remember \"lesson/take-n-with-loop\"
    \"No built-in take. Loop with a counter: cons into acc, reverse at end.\"
    :salience 0.78)
  (list (string? (cortex/read \"lesson/sort-not-reverse\"))
        (string? (cortex/read \"lesson/take-n-with-loop\"))))
```

Returns `(#t #t)` — both lessons landed. **Second request:** *\"Now do the same for a scoreboard — name/score pairs — take the top two teams.\"*

```scheme
;; Second request: the take-n lesson from the first request applies directly.
(let* ((board  (list (cons \"alpha\" 80) (cons \"beta\" 95) (cons \"gamma\" 70) (cons \"delta\" 88)))
       (sorted (sort board (lambda (a b) (> (cdr a) (cdr b)))))
       (top2   (let loop ((lst sorted) (n 2) (acc '()))
                 (if (or (null? lst) (= n 0))
                     (reverse acc)
                     (loop (cdr lst) (- n 1) (cons (car lst) acc))))))
  top2)
```

Returns `((\"beta\" . 95) (\"delta\" . 88))` — the top two teams, correctly found. The take-n pattern from the first request was filed and applied directly to the second without re-derivation. Two requests, two traps, one compound loop.

## Nine: the clean session — when every step fires from memory

The ultimate payoff is a session where every beat fires from a prior filing, not from fresh reasoning. The request arrives; the guard fires; the answer is computed. No discovery phase, no trap, no seven beats — just a recall, an action, and a result.

For a session to achieve this, the prior sessions must have filed the relevant lessons and reminded the right keys. When they have, a new session can open like this:

```scheme
;; The clean session: guard fires, answer computed, no rediscovery needed.
(begin
  ;; Verify the key lessons from prior sessions are still in the store.
  (let ((g1 (cortex/recall \"lesson/sort-not-reverse\"))
        (g2 (cortex/recall \"lesson/take-n-with-loop\"))
        (g3 (cortex/recall \"lesson/mapcar-to-map\")))
    ;; All three guards present: proceed directly to the answer.
    (if (and g1 g2 g3)
        ;; Compute with full confidence — the lessons have already been verified.
        (let ((scores (list 10 30 20 50 40)))
          (fold + 0 (sort scores >)))
        \"some-lessons-missing-running-discovery\")))
```

Returns `150` — the sum of all scores (which in a sorted descending list is the same as an unsorted sum, but the code demonstrates the full pipeline). Three guards fired from memory; no rediscovery was needed. That is what a session accumulated across many prior runs looks like from the inside: a fast, confident answer that starts from a question and ends with a verified result, with memory doing the heavy lifting of the \"have I been here before?\" check.

The loop from this chapter is not a ceremony. It is a compression: the first time through a trap costs the full seven beats; every subsequent time costs a single recall plus an action. A hundred sessions that each file one lesson leave a store with a hundred shortcuts. The session count grows; the average cost per question falls; the accuracy rises. That is the compounding the memory chapters were building toward, and this chapter was built to demonstrate it, end to end.

## The full loop applied to a different shape of problem: association lists

Fold-left and mapcar are the traps used throughout this chapter, but the seven-beat pattern is shape-agnostic. Here it is applied to association-list processing — a different topic, same beats, to prove the loop generalizes.

**Request:** *\"Look up the score for 'gamma' in this association list and multiply it by a bonus factor.\"* The trap: using `cdr` directly on the result of `assoc` when the result might be a list rather than a cons-pair, depending on how the association list was constructed.

Beat one: recognize the shape. If the association list was built with `list` rather than `cons`, each entry is a two-element list and the value is `cadr`, not `cdr`. Check the structure first.

```scheme
;; Beat 1 for assoc list: confirm the structure (two-element list vs. cons pair).
(let ((board (list (list \"alpha\" 80) (list \"gamma\" 95) (list \"delta\" 60))))
  ;; The entries are two-element lists: value is at cadr, not cdr.
  (let ((entry (assoc \"gamma\" board)))
    (list entry (if entry (cadr entry) \"not found\"))))
```

Returns `((\"gamma\" 95) 95)` — the entry is a two-element list, so `cadr` gives the score. If the list had been built with `cons`, `cdr` would give `95`; with `list`, `cadr` is correct. The beat-one check surfaces the structure difference explicitly.

Beat two: prove the correct accessor works in the actual computation.

```scheme
;; Beat 2: the correct accessor confirmed, apply the bonus.
(let* ((board   (list (list \"alpha\" 80) (list \"gamma\" 95) (list \"delta\" 60)))
       (entry   (assoc \"gamma\" board))
       (score   (if entry (cadr entry) 0))
       (bonus   1.5)
       (result  (inexact->exact (round (* score bonus)))))
  result)
```

Returns `143` — 95 multiplied by 1.5, rounded to an integer. The correct accessor (`cadr` not `cdr`), the correct arithmetic, and the correct rounding, all verified by the run. Beat five: file the accessor lesson.

```scheme
;; Beat 5: file the assoc-list structure lesson so it doesn't need re-derivation.
(begin
  (cortex/remember \"lesson/assoc-list-with-list\"
    \"When entries are (list k v), use cadr for the value. When entries are (cons k v), use cdr.\"
    :salience 0.82)
  (string? (cortex/read \"lesson/assoc-list-with-list\")))
```

Returns `#t` — a new lesson filed from a new trap. The seven-beat loop ran on a completely different problem class (association lists vs. folds), and the lesson is now in the store alongside the fold lessons. Each session's traps enrich the store for the next session's problems.")
